.TH DMUX 1 "September 2026" "dmux-ml" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dmux \- observe experiments in one terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmux
.RI [ command ] " [options]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
DMUX observes explicitly configured files, processes and scheduler reports.
Closing it leaves jobs running. Stops always require an exact typed label.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B home
Browse registered projects. Use --json for a state-only machine report.
.TP
.B watch
Open a plan. --project-root and --plan-dir select explicit locations.
.TP
.B json
Print a snapshot. --schema-version 1 retains legacy aliases; default is 2.
.TP
.B report
Compare a configured metric using --metric LABEL and optional --stage NAME.
--format markdown|csv selects output; --output PATH creates a new file.
Best values are limited to the bounded recent window.
.TP
.B settings list|get|set|reset
Manage validated preferences. Values use JSON syntax; strings may be unquoted.
.TP
.B timeline
Show opt-in observed transitions. --json prints the versioned event list.
.TP
.B demo --live
Launch tiny independent workloads in a fresh temporary project.
.TP
.B add, remove, projects
Manage registrations without changing experiment files or jobs.
.TP
.B init, doctor
Create a new plan or perform read-only connection diagnostics.
.TP
.B sessions, kill
Explicit session management and identity-checked process stop controls.
.TP
.B adapters, completions
List installed adapters or generate Bash/Zsh argcomplete shell code.
.SH KEYS
n/p select experiments; Enter opens details; l opens the log viewer;
c opens comparison; o opens options; ? shows help; q/Esc returns.
The log viewer supports j/k, Ctrl-D/U, / search and f follow.
.SH SETTINGS
Precedence is explicit CLI flag, DMUX_* environment variable, settings.json,
then default. Environment values use JSON syntax.
Settings are stored under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dmux, or ~/.config/dmux.
Opt-in transition history is stored under $XDG_STATE_HOME/dmux.
History describes observations, not unobserved activity between sessions.
--notify-cmd accepts an argv command, never a shell script. Event JSON is sent
on stdin only when state changes; delivery is bounded and asynchronous.
.SH PLATFORMS
Linux and macOS with Python 3.11 or newer. Native Windows is unsupported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR ssh (1)
